      "traceTextDiagram": "Session Capture Flow (skills/session_capture)\n├── Skill Entry Point\n│   └── skillmain.Main() \u003c-- 1a\n│       └── run() function \u003c-- skills/session_capture/main.go:53\n│           ├── Open Store\n│           │   └── sessionkit.OpenSessions() \u003c-- 1b\n│           │       └── sessions.Open() \u003c-- internal/sessionkit/open.go:20\n│           │           └── sqliteutil.OpenDB() \u003c-- internal/storage/sessions/store.go:95\n│           ├── Parse JSONL\n│           │   └── claudejsonl.OpenReader() \u003c-- 1c\n│           │       └── reader.ReadAll() \u003c-- skills/session_capture/main.go:112\n│           ├── Extract Metadata\n│           │   └── extractSession() \u003c-- 1d\n│           │       └── build Session struct \u003c-- skills/session_capture/main.go:214\n│           ├── Persist to Database\n│           │   └── sessionStore.Save() \u003c-- 1e\n│           │       └── Store.Save() \u003c-- internal/storage/sessions/store.go:135\n│           │           └── db.ExecContext(INSERT) \u003c-- internal/storage/sessions/store.go:178\n│           └── Return Result\n│               └── skillout.Emit() \u003c-- 1f",
      "traceGuide": "## Motivation\n\nClaude Code (Anthropic's IDE) saves conversation histories as **JSONL files** in `~/.claude/projects/`. These conversations contain valuable context about coding sessions—what was built, what decisions were made, what problems were encountered. However, this data sits in raw files, making it **impossible to search, analyze, or retrieve** past sessions efficiently.\n\nThe session capture system solves this by **extracting conversations from JSONL files and persisting them to a queryable database** with metadata, embeddings, and structured summaries. This enables semantic search across past work, context restoration for new sessions, and analytics on coding patterns.\n\n## Details\n\n### Capture Flow\n\nThe `session/capture` skill [1a] orchestrates the extraction process. It starts by **opening the sessions store** [1b]—a SQLite or Turso database that holds all captured sessions. The store is opened via `sessionkit.OpenSessions()`, which handles database initialization and schema migration automatically.\n\nNext, it **parses the JSONL file** [1c] using `claudejsonl.OpenReader()`. Claude Code's format contains messages with types like `user_request`, `assistant_response`, `tool_use`, and `compact_boundary`. The reader deserializes these into structured message objects.\n\nThe skill then **extracts metadata** [1d] by analyzing the parsed messages: counting user turns, tool invocations, and tokens; identifying the time range; and building a tools pattern string. This produces a `storage.Session` struct with all the high-level statistics.\n\n### Persistence\n\nThe session is **saved to the database** [1e] via `sessionStore.Save()`. This executes an `INSERT ... ON CONFLICT DO UPDATE` statement [at line 178], making the operation **idempotent**—running capture twice on the same session updates rather than duplicates.\n\nThe database schema includes:\n- **`sessions` table**: core metadata, embeddings, lineage tracking\n- **`session_turns` table**: individual conversation turns\n- **`session_chunks` table**: byte-offset indexed chunks for deep retrieval\n- **`session_context_windows` table**: compaction-bounded work spans\n\nFinally, the skill **returns a structured output** [1f] via `skillout.Emit()`, providing the session ID, message counts, and a preview of high-signal content. This output can be consumed by other skills or displayed to users.\n\n### Database Flexibility\n\nThe system supports **both SQLite (local) and Turso (cloud)** backends. Turso adds native vector search with `F32_BLOB` columns and vector indexes, enabling fast similarity queries for semantic retrieval. The store opening logic abstracts this—callers just use `sessions.Open()` and get the appropriate implementation based on configuration."
    },
